# Page smoke job budget: 20 -> 35 minutes

Apply with:

```sh
git apply docs/owner-patches/page-smoke-timeout.patch
```

## Why

The `smoke` job runs every browser suite one after another in a single job
with a 20-minute budget. When other browser suites run at the same time
(Navigation on main, or PR checks), the pool is busy and the job needs
longer than 20 minutes even when every check inside it is healthy.

Seen on 2026-11-02: the Page smoke run for main commit `a210530b` was
killed by this ceiling at 20m14s (attempt 2) while it was still making
healthy progress - `ios webkit ok` and `live page ok` logged immediately
before the cancel, no failing step. Its attempt 1 had failed on the
Local AI page's 10-second selector wait, a symptom of the same runner
contention; the same content passed on the previous main commit minutes
earlier in a quiet window. Under load the job has flaked before: on
2026-10-30 the same job failed at 13:49, 14:10, 14:27, 15:06 and 15:18
with passes between, on content that did not change.

The timeout is a cap, not a delay: a quiet-window job that finishes in
about 10 minutes still frees its runner at completion. The higher cap
only matters when the pool is busy, which is exactly when a false
timeout is most disruptive (it turns green checks into a red main).

